/* 50 principles and 200 lessons (100 middlegame, 100 endgame). Generated by tools/build_app_data.py */
const PRINCIPLES=[{"id":1,"f":"Opening","t":"Fight for the centre","r":"Put pawns and pieces where they control e4, d4, e5 and d5.","w":"Pieces in the centre reach both sides of the board. Pieces on the edge reach only one."},{"id":2,"f":"Opening","t":"Develop knights and bishops first","r":"Bring out the minor pieces before the queen and rooks.","w":"Minor pieces need one or two moves to become useful. Rooks only work once lines open."},{"id":3,"f":"Opening","t":"Don't move the same piece twice early","r":"Each opening move should add a new piece to the game.","w":"Every extra move with one piece is a tempo your opponent spends developing."},{"id":4,"f":"Opening","t":"Don't bring the queen out early","r":"The queen gets chased by smaller pieces and you lose time.","w":"Each time a knight or bishop attacks your queen, your opponent develops and you retreat."},{"id":5,"f":"Opening","t":"Castle early","r":"Get your king out of the centre within the first 10 or so moves.","w":"The centre opens fast. A king on e1 or e8 gets caught by checks along open files."},{"id":6,"f":"Opening","t":"Connect your rooks","r":"Finish development so the rooks see each other on the back rank.","w":"Connected rooks protect each other and can move to the open files."},{"id":7,"f":"Opening","t":"Don't grab pawns while behind in development","r":"A pawn is not worth three tempi.","w":"Material won early is often given back with interest when the opponent attacks your undeveloped camp."},{"id":8,"f":"Opening","t":"Few pawn moves in the opening","r":"Move only the pawns that free your pieces or fight for the centre.","w":"Pawns can't move backwards. Every pawn move leaves squares behind it weaker forever."},{"id":9,"f":"Opening","t":"Know the plans, not just the moves","r":"Learn why each move is played and what to do when the book ends.","w":"Opponents leave your preparation early. Understanding the plan tells you what to do next."},{"id":10,"f":"Opening","t":"Watch f7 and f2","r":"The square next to the king, guarded only by the king, is the weakest point early on.","w":"Many quick wins start with a hit on f7 or f2 (the Fried Liver, Scholar's Mate, the Evans)."},{"id":11,"f":"Strategy","t":"Improve your worst piece","r":"When you don't know what to do, find your least active piece and give it a job.","w":"A position is only as strong as its weakest link. One idle piece means you're a piece down in the fight."},{"id":12,"f":"Strategy","t":"Knights need outposts","r":"Put knights on protected squares in enemy territory that pawns can't attack.","w":"A knight on an outpost (like d5 or e6) can be worth more than a rook."},{"id":13,"f":"Strategy","t":"Bishops need open diagonals","r":"Open the position for your bishops. Keep your pawns off your bishop's colour.","w":"A bishop blocked by its own pawns is 'bad' and plays like a big pawn."},{"id":14,"f":"Strategy","t":"Rooks belong on open files","r":"Put rooks on files with no pawns, or on files that are about to open.","w":"A rook on a closed file is asleep. On an open file it can invade."},{"id":15,"f":"Strategy","t":"Rook on the seventh rank","r":"A rook (or two) on the 7th rank attacks pawns and traps the king.","w":"Pawns on their starting rank can only be defended sideways. The rook eats them."},{"id":16,"f":"Strategy","t":"Attack where you have more space","r":"Your pawn chain points the way: play on the side it points towards.","w":"More space means your pieces can move there more easily than the defender's."},{"id":17,"f":"Strategy","t":"Play on the side where you're stronger","r":"Count attackers against defenders. Attack where you have more.","w":"An attack with fewer pieces than the defence fails. Bring more pieces first."},{"id":18,"f":"Strategy","t":"Create and target weaknesses","r":"Fix weak pawns and squares, then pile up on them.","w":"Isolated, doubled and backward pawns can't defend themselves. They are targets for the whole game."},{"id":19,"f":"Strategy","t":"Two weaknesses win","r":"One weakness can be defended. Attack a second one on the other side.","w":"The defender can't cover both sides at once, so something breaks."},{"id":20,"f":"Strategy","t":"Trade when ahead, avoid trades when behind","r":"Material up: swap pieces, not pawns. Material down: keep pieces on.","w":"With fewer pieces, an extra pawn or piece counts for more, and counterplay disappears."},{"id":21,"f":"Strategy","t":"Prophylaxis: ask what your opponent wants","r":"Before your plan, find his idea and stop it if it is dangerous.","w":"Petrosian and Karpov won by taking away the opponent's best plan before it started."},{"id":22,"f":"Strategy","t":"Don't open the position when your king is weaker","r":"Opening lines helps the side with the safer king and more active pieces.","w":"Open files and diagonals are highways. Make sure they lead to his king, not yours."},{"id":23,"f":"Tactics","t":"Checks, captures, threats first","r":"Look at every forcing move, for both sides, before anything else.","w":"Forcing moves limit the replies. They are where combinations and blunders live."},{"id":24,"f":"Tactics","t":"Loose pieces drop off","r":"Undefended pieces are targets for forks, skewers and double attacks.","w":"Most tactics hit two things at once. A loose piece is half of that."},{"id":25,"f":"Tactics","t":"Blunder check every move","r":"After choosing a move, list all his checks, captures and threats against it.","w":"It takes ten seconds and saves most lost games."},{"id":26,"f":"Tactics","t":"Is the square safe?","r":"Before you move a piece, ask whether it can be taken or attacked there.","w":"1 in 6 of your blunders hung the piece you just moved."},{"id":27,"f":"Tactics","t":"What did my piece stop defending?","r":"A moving piece can leave another piece or square undefended.","w":"Deflection and overload tactics punish pieces that do two jobs."},{"id":28,"f":"Tactics","t":"The king's position invites tactics","r":"An exposed or badly placed king (and back rank) is where combinations appear.","w":"Checks gain time. Mates end the game."},{"id":29,"f":"Tactics","t":"Double attack","r":"Attack two targets with one move: forks, discovered attacks, queen double attacks.","w":"The opponent can only parry one threat at a time."},{"id":30,"f":"Tactics","t":"Pins and skewers on lines","r":"Line up enemy pieces on a file, rank or diagonal and attack through them.","w":"A pinned piece can't move (or can't without losing more). A skewer wins what's behind."},{"id":31,"f":"Tactics","t":"Remove the defender","r":"Capture or deflect the piece that guards the key square or piece.","w":"Many 'impossible' moves become possible once the defender is gone."},{"id":32,"f":"Tactics","t":"In-between moves","r":"Before recapturing, check whether a check or threat comes first.","w":"An intermezzo changes the whole calculation and is easy to miss."},{"id":33,"f":"Tactics","t":"Calculate to the end, then evaluate","r":"Follow forcing lines until they stop, then count material and king safety.","w":"Stopping one move early is how sacrifices fail and blunders happen."},{"id":34,"f":"Tactics","t":"Quiet moves can be the strongest","r":"Sometimes the winning move is not a check or capture.","w":"Fischer's 19.Rf6 and Rubinstein's ...Rd2 were quiet, and decisive."},{"id":35,"f":"Defence","t":"When attacked, counterattack if you can","r":"The best defence often creates a bigger threat.","w":"Passive defence lets the attacker add pieces at leisure."},{"id":36,"f":"Defence","t":"Give back material to stop an attack","r":"Returning a pawn or the exchange can kill the initiative.","w":"A safe king a pawn down is better than a dead king a piece up."},{"id":37,"f":"Defence","t":"Initiative and time matter as much as material","r":"Count tempi and active pieces, not just points.","w":"Tal won game after game a pawn or a piece down because his pieces were working."},{"id":38,"f":"Defence","t":"Use your clock at critical moments","r":"Spend time on captures, sacrifices, pawn breaks and when the position changes.","w":"Moving fast in quiet moments is fine. Moving fast at turning points loses games."},{"id":39,"f":"Defence","t":"When winning, simplify and stay safe","r":"Fischer mode: trade pieces, cut counterplay, double your blunder check.","w":"30 of your 104 blunders came in won positions."},{"id":40,"f":"Defence","t":"When losing, make it complicated","r":"Create threats and unbalance the position. Opponents relax too.","w":"Under pressure and with a clock running, even strong players err."},{"id":41,"f":"Endgame","t":"Activate the king","r":"In the endgame the king is a fighting piece. Bring it to the centre.","w":"With queens off, the king is safe and worth about a minor piece in activity."},{"id":42,"f":"Endgame","t":"Passed pawns must be pushed","r":"A passed pawn ties down enemy pieces. Push it when it is safe.","w":"Every step forward makes it more dangerous and harder to stop."},{"id":43,"f":"Endgame","t":"Rooks behind passed pawns","r":"Your rook goes behind your passed pawn, and behind the enemy's passed pawn too.","w":"From behind, the rook gains scope as the pawn advances. In front, it gets passive."},{"id":44,"f":"Endgame","t":"The opposition","r":"In king and pawn endings, kings facing each other with one square between: the side not to move holds it.","w":"Taking the opposition decides who gets to advance and who must give way."},{"id":45,"f":"Endgame","t":"The rule of the square","r":"If the king can step into the pawn's square, it catches the pawn.","w":"A quick count that avoids long calculation in pawn races."},{"id":46,"f":"Endgame","t":"Rook endings: activity beats material","r":"An active rook is often worth a pawn. Avoid passive defence.","w":"Capablanca and Rubinstein gave pawns to activate their rooks and kings."},{"id":47,"f":"Endgame","t":"Know the key drawn and won positions","r":"Lucena (win), Philidor (draw), wrong bishop (draw), and K+P key squares.","w":"These positions decide what you aim for when you trade into an endgame."},{"id":48,"f":"Endgame","t":"Bishop vs knight: pawns on both wings favour the bishop","r":"Knights are good in closed positions with pawns on one side.","w":"The bishop is long-range. The knight is short-range but can reach every square colour."},{"id":49,"f":"Endgame","t":"Don't rush: improve first, then break","r":"In won endgames, improve every piece before the decisive pawn push.","w":"There is no hurry. The defender can't improve, so take the time."},{"id":50,"f":"Endgame","t":"Create a passed pawn with a majority","r":"A pawn majority on one wing can produce a passed pawn: candidate pawn first.","w":"The outside passed pawn decoys the enemy king while yours eats the other side."}];
const LESSONS=[{"id":"mid-1","part":"mid","n":1,"m":"Thinking and safety","t":"The blunder check","p":[25,23],"x":"Before you press a piece, stop and look at the board from your opponent's side. After your move, which checks can he give? Which captures? What does he threaten? Most blunders are not deep. They are one-move oversights by someone who didn't look.","do":"Say 'checks, captures, threats' in your head before every move.","dont":"Don't move the moment you see a good idea. That's exactly when you stop looking.","pr":"set:defence"},{"id":"mid-2","part":"mid","n":2,"m":"Thinking and safety","t":"What does his last move want?","p":[21,23],"x":"Every move your opponent makes has a purpose. Before thinking about your own plan, ask what his move attacks, what it opens, and what it stopped defending. A quarter of your blunders ignored a threat that was already on the board.","do":"Start every think with: 'Why did he play that?'","dont":"Don't assume his move is harmless because it looks quiet.","pr":"set:defence"},{"id":"mid-3","part":"mid","n":3,"m":"Thinking and safety","t":"Is the square safe?","p":[26],"x":"When you move a piece, check the destination square. Can a pawn or a cheaper piece take it? Is it attacked more times than it's defended? 1 in 6 of your blunders hung the very piece you just moved.","do":"Count attackers and defenders of the square you're moving to.","dont":"Don't put pieces where a pawn can kick them with tempo.","pr":"theme:hangingPiece"},{"id":"mid-4","part":"mid","n":4,"m":"Thinking and safety","t":"Loose pieces drop off","p":[24],"x":"A loose piece is one that nobody defends. It costs nothing until a double attack comes, and then you lose it. Strong players keep their pieces defended almost all the time.","do":"After each move, count your undefended pieces. Try to keep it at zero or one.","dont":"Don't leave a bishop or knight undefended on a square a queen can reach.","pr":"theme:hangingPiece"},{"id":"mid-5","part":"mid","n":5,"m":"Thinking and safety","t":"What did my piece stop defending?","p":[27],"x":"Pieces have jobs. When a piece moves, its old job is abandoned. A knight leaving f6 may stop guarding h7, and a rook leaving the back rank may allow mate. Check what you're giving up, not only what you're getting.","do":"Before moving a defender, ask: 'Who was it protecting?'","dont":"Don't move your only defender of the back rank.","pr":"theme:deflection"},{"id":"mid-6","part":"mid","n":6,"m":"Thinking and safety","t":"Candidate moves","p":[33],"x":"Before calculating, list two or three reasonable moves. Then compare them. This stops you falling in love with the first idea and gives you a fallback if your main line fails.","do":"Write or say your candidates, then check each one.","dont":"Don't calculate one move for five minutes and then play a different, unchecked move.","pr":"set:deep"},{"id":"mid-7","part":"mid","n":7,"m":"Thinking and safety","t":"Improve your worst piece","p":[11],"x":"When there are no tactics and no obvious plan, find your worst piece. Is a bishop blocked, a knight on the rim, or a rook still on its starting square? Moving it to a better square is almost always a good move.","do":"Ask 'Which piece is doing nothing?' at every quiet moment.","dont":"Don't make random pawn moves because you have no plan.","pr":"game:fischer-petrosian-1971"},{"id":"mid-8","part":"mid","n":8,"m":"Thinking and safety","t":"Use your clock at the right moments","p":[38],"x":"You use only 14% of your clock by move 15 in rapid, and none of your rapid mistakes came in time trouble. Spend your time at turning points: captures, sacrifices, pawn breaks, and when the opponent changes the position.","do":"Take an extra minute whenever the position changes character.","dont":"Don't play instantly just because the move looks natural."},{"id":"mid-9","part":"mid","n":9,"m":"Thinking and safety","t":"Forcing moves first, in order","p":[23],"x":"Look at checks first (they leave the fewest replies), then captures, then threats. Do it for both sides. Most combinations start with a check or capture, and most of your blunders were punished by one.","do":"Scan checks, then captures, then threats, every move.","dont":"Don't skip the scan in 'boring' positions.","pr":"set:core"},{"id":"mid-10","part":"mid","n":10,"m":"Thinking and safety","t":"Try to refute your own move","p":[25,33],"x":"Once you've chosen a move, switch sides and try to beat it. Look for his best reply, not the one you hope for. If you can't find a good answer for him, play it.","do":"Ask 'What is his best answer?' not 'What will he probably play?'","dont":"Don't play 'hope chess', a move that only works if he blunders.","pr":"set:defence"},{"id":"mid-11","part":"mid","n":11,"m":"Tactical motifs","t":"The knight fork","p":[29,24],"x":"A knight can attack two pieces at once, and nothing can block it. Look for squares where your knight hits the king and another piece, or the queen and a rook. Knights fork in an L-shape, so the targets are usually on the same-coloured squares.","do":"Check which squares your knight can reach in two moves. Look for juicy targets.","dont":"Don't put your king and queen a knight's jump apart from the same square.","pr":"theme:fork"},{"id":"mid-12","part":"mid","n":12,"m":"Tactical motifs","t":"The queen fork","p":[29],"x":"The queen moves in eight directions, so she often attacks two loose pieces at once, especially with check. Many 'quiet' blunders lose to a queen move that hits a loose piece and the king.","do":"Look for queen checks that also attack something.","dont":"Don't leave pieces loose on the same line as your king.","pr":"theme:fork"},{"id":"mid-13","part":"mid","n":13,"m":"Tactical motifs","t":"The pawn fork","p":[29],"x":"A pawn attacks two diagonal squares, so pushing it can attack two pieces at once. Because a pawn is the cheapest unit, any fork with it wins material.","do":"Watch for pieces standing side by side on your opponent's third rank.","dont":"Don't put two pieces next to each other in front of enemy pawns.","pr":"theme:fork"},{"id":"mid-14","part":"mid","n":14,"m":"Tactical motifs","t":"Absolute pins","p":[30],"x":"When a piece stands in front of its king, it can't move at all. That's an absolute pin. Attack the pinned piece again (with a pawn if you can) to win it.","do":"Pile up on a pinned piece.","dont":"Don't leave your king on the same diagonal as an enemy bishop with a piece in between.","pr":"theme:pin"},{"id":"mid-15","part":"mid","n":15,"m":"Tactical motifs","t":"Relative pins","p":[30],"x":"When a piece stands in front of the queen or a rook, moving it loses the more valuable piece behind. Such a piece can legally move, but usually shouldn't.","do":"Ask what stands behind a piece before attacking it.","dont":"Don't line up your queen behind a minor piece on an open line.","pr":"theme:pin"},{"id":"mid-16","part":"mid","n":16,"m":"Tactical motifs","t":"Breaking a pin","p":[30,35],"x":"To escape a pin, you can step the king or queen out of the line, put a defender between, or attack the pinning piece (often with ...h6 and ...g5). Kick the pinning piece before it gets reinforced.","do":"Break pins early, before the attacker piles up.","dont":"Don't play ...h6 and ...g5 if it wrecks your king's cover.","pr":"theme:pin"},{"id":"mid-17","part":"mid","n":17,"m":"Tactical motifs","t":"The skewer","p":[30],"x":"A skewer is a pin in reverse. You attack a valuable piece, it moves, and you take what was behind it. Rooks and bishops skewer along lines, often with check.","do":"Look for checks along a line with a loose piece behind the king.","dont":"Don't leave your king and queen on the same open line.","pr":"theme:skewer"},{"id":"mid-18","part":"mid","n":18,"m":"Tactical motifs","t":"Discovered attacks","p":[29],"x":"Move one piece out of the way and the piece behind it attacks. The moving piece can make a second threat at the same time. Two threats in one move is very hard to meet.","do":"Look for your pieces lined up behind your own pieces, aimed at targets.","dont":"Don't ignore an enemy rook or bishop hidden behind one of his own pieces.","pr":"theme:discoveredAttack"},{"id":"mid-19","part":"mid","n":19,"m":"Tactical motifs","t":"Discovered check and double check","p":[28,29],"x":"If the uncovered piece gives check, the moving piece can go anywhere, even to a square where it can be taken. In a double check, both pieces give check, and the king must move.","do":"When you have a discovered check ready, find the most damaging move for the other piece.","dont":"Don't allow a bishop-and-rook battery aimed at your king.","pr":"theme:discoveredAttack"},{"id":"mid-20","part":"mid","n":20,"m":"Tactical motifs","t":"Deflection","p":[31],"x":"Force a defender to leave its post, often with a check or a capture it must answer. Then the square or piece it guarded falls.","do":"Find the piece doing a defensive job, and give it a reason to leave.","dont":"Don't rely on one piece to guard two things.","pr":"theme:deflection"},{"id":"mid-21","part":"mid","n":21,"m":"Tactical motifs","t":"Attraction (decoy)","p":[31,28],"x":"Lure a piece, often the king, onto a bad square with a sacrifice. Then a fork, check or mate follows. Lasker's 15.Bxh7+ is a famous decoy.","do":"Ask: 'If his king stood on this square, would I have a fork or mate?'","dont":"Don't take every sacrifice offered. Check what follows.","pr":"theme:attraction"},{"id":"mid-22","part":"mid","n":22,"m":"Tactical motifs","t":"Overloaded pieces","p":[27,31],"x":"A piece that defends two things can't do both. Attack one, and when it recaptures, the other falls.","do":"Count how many jobs each enemy piece has.","dont":"Don't give your own pieces two jobs at once.","pr":"theme:deflection"},{"id":"mid-23","part":"mid","n":23,"m":"Tactical motifs","t":"Removing the defender","p":[31],"x":"Capture the piece that protects the key square or piece, even if it costs material. Then take what it protected.","do":"Find the defender of his weak point and remove it.","dont":"Don't forget that your capture may itself remove one of your defenders.","pr":"theme:deflection"},{"id":"mid-24","part":"mid","n":24,"m":"Tactical motifs","t":"Interference","p":[31],"x":"Put a piece between an enemy piece and the square it defends, cutting the line.","do":"Look for squares where your piece blocks two enemy lines at once.","dont":"Don't rely on long-range defenders through squares the enemy can occupy.","pr":"set:deep"},{"id":"mid-25","part":"mid","n":25,"m":"Tactical motifs","t":"Clearance","p":[29],"x":"Move or sacrifice your own piece to clear a square or line for another piece.","do":"If a great square is occupied by your own piece, ask how to clear it with tempo.","dont":"Don't overlook moves that clear a line for a rook or bishop.","pr":"theme:clearance"},{"id":"mid-26","part":"mid","n":26,"m":"Tactical motifs","t":"X-ray attacks","p":[30],"x":"A rook or bishop can attack through an enemy piece to a square behind it. When that piece moves or is exchanged, the x-ray comes alive.","do":"Line up behind enemy pieces on files and diagonals.","dont":"Don't trade on a square where the recapture opens his x-ray.","pr":"theme:xRayAttack"},{"id":"mid-27","part":"mid","n":27,"m":"Tactical motifs","t":"In-between moves (zwischenzug)","p":[32],"x":"Before making the 'obvious' recapture, check whether a check or bigger threat comes first. Your opponent must answer it, then you recapture with an improved position.","do":"Before every recapture, scan for checks and threats.","dont":"Don't assume your opponent must recapture right away.","pr":"theme:intermezzo"},{"id":"mid-28","part":"mid","n":28,"m":"Tactical motifs","t":"Trapped pieces","p":[24],"x":"A piece with no safe squares can be won by attacking it. Bishops on a2 or h7 (after ...b5 or g4 pawn advances) and knights on the rim are typical victims.","do":"Before grabbing a pawn with a piece, check it can get back out.","dont":"Don't send your bishop deep into enemy pawns (Bxh7 or Bxa7) without an escape route.","pr":"theme:trappedPiece"},{"id":"mid-29","part":"mid","n":29,"m":"Tactical motifs","t":"Desperado","p":[32],"x":"When a piece is lost anyway, use it to take as much as possible first.","do":"If your piece is doomed, find its most expensive capture.","dont":"Don't just let a doomed piece be taken for free.","pr":"set:deep"},{"id":"mid-30","part":"mid","n":30,"m":"Tactical motifs","t":"The back-rank mate","p":[28],"x":"A king behind its own unmoved pawns can be mated by a rook or queen on the back rank. Bobby Fischer Teaches Chess drills this pattern hundreds of times.","do":"Make 'luft' (h3 or g3) once the middlegame calms down.","dont":"Don't let your back rank be guarded by only one piece.","pr":"theme:backRankMate"},{"id":"mid-31","part":"mid","n":31,"m":"Tactical motifs","t":"Smothered mate","p":[28],"x":"A knight mates a king that is boxed in by its own pieces, often after a queen sacrifice that forces a rook to block.","do":"Remember the pattern: Qg8+ Rxg8, Nf7 mate.","dont":"Don't let your king be surrounded by your own pieces with knights around.","pr":"theme:smotheredMate"},{"id":"mid-32","part":"mid","n":32,"m":"Tactical motifs","t":"The Greek gift (Bxh7+)","p":[28,37],"x":"Bxh7+ Kxh7, Ng5+ and Qh5 is the classic attack on a castled king. It works when the bishop and knight can join, the queen reaches h5, and Black can't defend with ...Nf6 or ...Bf5.","do":"Check three conditions: Bd3 aimed at h7, a knight that reaches g5, and the queen to h5.","dont":"Don't sacrifice if Black's king can run to g6 safely or a knight covers h7.","pr":"theme:sacrifice"},{"id":"mid-33","part":"mid","n":33,"m":"Tactical motifs","t":"Mating patterns: Anastasia, Arabian, Boden","p":[28],"x":"Knowing mating patterns lets you see mates instantly. Arabian: rook and knight in the corner. Anastasia: knight and rook against a king on the edge. Boden: two bishops on crossing diagonals.","do":"Learn one mating pattern a week by solving mate puzzles.","dont":"Don't just calculate. Recognise.","pr":"theme:mateIn2"},{"id":"mid-34","part":"mid","n":34,"m":"Tactical motifs","t":"Perpetual check","p":[40],"x":"If you're losing, look for endless checks: a draw by repetition. The queen is the best perpetual-check piece.","do":"When worse, count how many squares his king can hide on.","dont":"Don't let your king become open to endless queen checks when you're winning.","pr":"theme:mateIn3"},{"id":"mid-35","part":"mid","n":35,"m":"Tactical motifs","t":"Stalemate tricks","p":[40,39],"x":"A king with no legal moves, and no other moves available, is stalemate: a draw. Losing players aim for it by sacrificing their last pieces.","do":"When winning, always leave the enemy king a square.","dont":"Don't take the last free piece if it leaves his king with no moves."},{"id":"mid-36","part":"mid","n":36,"m":"Attacking the king","t":"Attack with more pieces than defenders","p":[17],"x":"Count the attackers near his king and the defenders. An attack with two pieces against three defenders usually fails. Bring a rook or knight first.","do":"Add one more attacker before starting the sacrifices.","dont":"Don't attack with the queen alone.","pr":"theme:kingsideAttack"},{"id":"mid-37","part":"mid","n":37,"m":"Attacking the king","t":"Opposite-side castling: race with pawns","p":[16,22],"x":"When kings castle on opposite sides, push the pawns in front of the enemy king. You don't weaken your own king by doing so. Speed decides the race.","do":"Count moves: whose attack arrives first?","dont":"Don't make defensive moves in a race unless forced.","pr":"game:tal-larsen-1965"},{"id":"mid-38","part":"mid","n":38,"m":"Attacking the king","t":"Same-side castling: attack with pieces","p":[16,22],"x":"When both kings are on the same side, pushing your own pawns weakens your own king. Attack with pieces (knight to g5, queen to h5, rook lifts) and use the pawns sparingly.","do":"Use rook lifts (Re3-h3) to add attackers.","dont":"Don't push g4 and h4 in front of your own castled king without a clear reason.","pr":"game:fischer-myagmarsuren-1967"},{"id":"mid-39","part":"mid","n":39,"m":"Attacking the king","t":"Open the h-file against a fianchetto","p":[14,28],"x":"Against ...g6 and ...Bg7, push h4-h5 and swap on g6 to open the h-file, and trade the g7 bishop with Bh6. That's the Yugoslav and 150 Attack plan.","do":"Trade the fianchetto bishop first. Then the dark squares are weak.","dont":"Don't open the h-file if your own king is on the kingside.","pr":"game:kasparov-topalov-1999"},{"id":"mid-40","part":"mid","n":40,"m":"Attacking the king","t":"Sacrifices on h7, g7, f7 and e6","p":[28,37],"x":"The squares around the castled king are where sacrifices happen. Before you sacrifice, check that your follow-up pieces arrive with tempo.","do":"Calculate the checks after the sacrifice.","dont":"Don't sacrifice for 'activity' when the defender gets time to consolidate.","pr":"theme:sacrifice"},{"id":"mid-41","part":"mid","n":41,"m":"Attacking the king","t":"Rook lifts","p":[14,17],"x":"A rook can reach the attack via the third rank (Re1-e3-h3 or Rf3-h3). It's often the missing attacker.","do":"Check whether your rook can swing to the kingside in two moves.","dont":"Don't leave your back rank undefended when lifting.","pr":"game:lasker-bauer-1889"},{"id":"mid-42","part":"mid","n":42,"m":"Attacking the king","t":"Attack when the centre is closed","p":[16,22],"x":"With the centre locked, flank attacks are safe because the opponent can't counter in the middle. Push pawns on the side your chain points to (KID: ...f5, ...g5).","do":"Lock the centre first, then attack.","dont":"Don't attack on the wing while the centre can still open.","pr":"theme:kingsideAttack"},{"id":"mid-43","part":"mid","n":43,"m":"Attacking the king","t":"Answer a wing attack with a central break","p":[22,35],"x":"The classic defence against a flank attack is a counter-blow in the centre. It opens lines towards the attacker's own king.","do":"When he storms your king, look for ...d5 or ...c5 breaks.","dont":"Don't defend passively while his pawns roll.","pr":"set:defence"},{"id":"mid-44","part":"mid","n":44,"m":"Attacking the king","t":"Punish a king left in the centre","p":[5,22],"x":"If the enemy king hasn't castled, open the centre: pawn breaks, rooks to e1 and d1, sacrifices on e6 or f7. The Opera Game and Fischer-Fine show how fast it ends.","do":"Count: is his king stuck? Then open files.","dont":"Don't let your own king stay on e1 'one more move'.","pr":"game:morphy-opera-1858"},{"id":"mid-45","part":"mid","n":45,"m":"Attacking the king","t":"The initiative","p":[37],"x":"Having the initiative means you make threats and he answers. Keep it: every move should ask a question. If you stop, his pieces come back.","do":"Make the next threat before he finishes defending.","dont":"Don't spend a tempo on a slow move while you have the initiative.","pr":"game:fischer-byrne-1956"},{"id":"mid-46","part":"mid","n":46,"m":"Attacking the king","t":"Attacking pawns: the battering ram","p":[16],"x":"Advance pawns to open lines: g4-g5 kicks the defending knight, h4-h5 opens a file. Each pawn advance should make a threat.","do":"Push with tempo, kicking defenders.","dont":"Don't push pawns that only create holes in your camp.","pr":"theme:kingsideAttack"},{"id":"mid-47","part":"mid","n":47,"m":"Attacking the king","t":"The queen and knight team","p":[28,29],"x":"Queen and knight together are the best attacking pair. The knight covers the squares the queen can't.","do":"Bring the knight close to his king (f5, g5, h5) with the queen behind.","dont":"Don't trade off your attacking knight for a passive piece.","pr":"theme:kingsideAttack"},{"id":"mid-48","part":"mid","n":48,"m":"Attacking the king","t":"Exploit dark or light square weaknesses","p":[13,18],"x":"If the fianchetto bishop is gone, the squares of its colour around the king are holes. Your queen and bishop invade them.","do":"Trade the bishop that guards his king's colour.","dont":"Don't trade your own bishop that guards your king's holes.","pr":"set:mate"},{"id":"mid-49","part":"mid","n":49,"m":"Attacking the king","t":"Mate in two and three","p":[23,28],"x":"Short mates are found by trying every check. In mate puzzles the solution almost always starts with a check or a sacrifice.","do":"Try every check, even the 'impossible' ones.","dont":"Don't stop at the first check that fails. Try them all.","pr":"theme:mateIn2"},{"id":"mid-50","part":"mid","n":50,"m":"Attacking the king","t":"When the attack is over, switch plans","p":[17,20],"x":"If the defender has held and your attack has no new pieces to add, stop. Convert to an endgame, or switch to the other wing.","do":"Ask: 'Can I add any more attackers?' If not, change plans.","dont":"Don't keep sacrificing in a dead attack."},{"id":"mid-51","part":"mid","n":51,"m":"Defence","t":"Defend by counterattacking","p":[35],"x":"The strongest defence often makes a bigger threat of your own. If your counter-threat is faster, his attack has to stop.","do":"When attacked, first look for your own checks and threats.","dont":"Don't retreat everything to the back rank.","pr":"set:defence"},{"id":"mid-52","part":"mid","n":52,"m":"Defence","t":"Trade the attacking pieces","p":[20,35],"x":"Every piece traded off near your king reduces the attack. Offer exchanges of his most dangerous attacker.","do":"Trade his queen or his best attacking minor piece.","dont":"Don't trade your own defenders for his passive pieces.","pr":"set:defence"},{"id":"mid-53","part":"mid","n":53,"m":"Defence","t":"Give material back to kill the attack","p":[36],"x":"A pawn or the exchange returned at the right moment often ends the attack completely. Tal's opponents who clung to material usually lost.","do":"Return material if it trades queens or stops mate threats.","dont":"Don't hold on to an extra pawn while your king burns.","pr":"set:defence"},{"id":"mid-54","part":"mid","n":54,"m":"Defence","t":"Prophylaxis: stop his plan before it starts","p":[21],"x":"Ask what your opponent wants to do next, and play a move that makes it impossible. Petrosian made a career of it.","do":"Ask 'What's his best move if it were his turn now?'","dont":"Don't make your plan without checking his.","pr":"game:petrosian-pachman-1961"},{"id":"mid-55","part":"mid","n":55,"m":"Defence","t":"Don't push the pawns in front of your king","p":[22,8],"x":"Every pawn move in front of your castled king creates holes the enemy pieces can use. Move them only when there's a concrete reason.","do":"Keep f2, g2 and h2 (or f7, g7 and h7) in place unless forced.","dont":"Don't play ...g5 or ...h6 'to gain space' near your own king."},{"id":"mid-56","part":"mid","n":56,"m":"Defence","t":"Make luft at the right time","p":[28],"x":"One small pawn move (h3 or ...h6) gives your king an escape square from back-rank mates. Do it when the position is calm.","do":"Play h3 or ...h6 once development is done and the back rank is a concern.","dont":"Don't make luft if it creates a target for a pawn storm.","pr":"theme:backRankMate"},{"id":"mid-57","part":"mid","n":57,"m":"Defence","t":"Defending against Bxh7+","p":[28],"x":"The Greek gift fails when a knight guards h7 (from f6) or the king can safely go to g6. Keep a knight on f6 when White's bishop aims at h7.","do":"Keep ...Nf6 in place while White's bishop is on d3.","dont":"Don't play ...Nf6-d5 or ...Nf6-e4 casually with Bd3 and Qd1-h5 possible.","pr":"theme:sacrifice"},{"id":"mid-58","part":"mid","n":58,"m":"Defence","t":"Defend with tempo","p":[35],"x":"The best defensive moves also attack something: they defend and gain time.","do":"Look for defensive moves that hit a loose enemy piece.","dont":"Don't play purely passive moves if a defending move with a threat exists.","pr":"set:defence"},{"id":"mid-59","part":"mid","n":59,"m":"Defence","t":"Fortresses","p":[36],"x":"When material is down, build a position the opponent can't break: pawns on one colour, a blockading piece, the king on a safe square.","do":"When losing, ask: 'Can I make a wall he can't break?'","dont":"Don't open the position when you're down material."},{"id":"mid-60","part":"mid","n":60,"m":"Defence","t":"Stay calm when worse","p":[40],"x":"Most games are not lost by the first mistake but by the panic that follows. Find the most resilient move, and keep giving problems.","do":"In bad positions, play the most stubborn move, not the most hopeful.","dont":"Don't resign in daily chess. Your opponents blunder too.","pr":"set:defence"},{"id":"mid-61","part":"mid","n":61,"m":"Pawn structures","t":"The isolated queen's pawn (playing with it)","p":[16,37],"x":"An isolated d-pawn gives space and open files for pieces. The side with it should attack: Ne5, Bd3 and Qd3, a rook lift, and the d4-d5 break.","do":"Keep pieces on and attack the kingside.","dont":"Don't trade pieces. The endgame favours the defender.","pr":"theme:kingsideAttack"},{"id":"mid-62","part":"mid","n":62,"m":"Pawn structures","t":"The isolated queen's pawn (playing against it)","p":[18,20],"x":"Against the IQP: blockade the square in front (a knight on d5 or d4), trade pieces, and win the pawn in the endgame. That's Fischer's recipe.","do":"Blockade with a knight, then trade.","dont":"Don't let the pawn advance with d4-d5.","pr":"game:fischer-petrosian-1971"},{"id":"mid-63","part":"mid","n":63,"m":"Pawn structures","t":"Hanging pawns","p":[18],"x":"Two pawns side by side on c and d with no pawns beside them are strong while they stand together, and weak once one is forced forward.","do":"Against hanging pawns: pressure them with rooks and pieces to force one forward.","dont":"Don't let them advance with tempo.","pr":"game:fischer-spassky-1972"},{"id":"mid-64","part":"mid","n":64,"m":"Pawn structures","t":"Doubled pawns","p":[18],"x":"Doubled pawns are weak in endgames, but they often open files for rooks and control extra central squares. Judge them case by case.","do":"Count what you gained (open file, bishop pair) against the weakness.","dont":"Don't double your pawns without getting something back."},{"id":"mid-65","part":"mid","n":65,"m":"Pawn structures","t":"Backward pawns","p":[18,12],"x":"A backward pawn can't be supported by other pawns, and the square in front of it is a hole. Put a piece on that hole.","do":"Occupy the square in front of a backward pawn.","dont":"Don't let your own pawn become backward on a half-open file."},{"id":"mid-66","part":"mid","n":66,"m":"Pawn structures","t":"Pawn chains: attack the base","p":[16],"x":"In a pawn chain (e.g. d4-e5 against d5-e6), attack the base of the enemy chain: ...c5 against d4, or f3 against e4. The side the chain points to is your attacking side.","do":"Find the base of his chain and hit it.","dont":"Don't attack the head of the chain. It's protected."},{"id":"mid-67","part":"mid","n":67,"m":"Pawn structures","t":"The Carlsbad structure and the minority attack","p":[18,19],"x":"After cxd5 exd5 in the Queen's Gambit, White's queenside pawns (two against three) advance with b4-b5 to leave Black with a weak c-pawn.","do":"Plan: Rb1, b4, b5, then target c6.","dont":"Don't start the minority attack before Black's queenside counterplay is controlled.","pr":"game:fischer-spassky-1972"},{"id":"mid-68","part":"mid","n":68,"m":"Pawn structures","t":"The Caro-Kann and Slav structure","p":[13,18],"x":"With pawns on c6 and d5, Black is solid. The light bishop comes out before ...e6. Plans are ...c5 or ...e5 breaks, and trading pieces.","do":"Get the bishop to f5 or g4 before playing ...e6.","dont":"Don't play ...c5 while White's pieces aim at your king (Carlsen-Ernst).","pr":"game:carlsen-ernst-2004"},{"id":"mid-69","part":"mid","n":69,"m":"Pawn structures","t":"The French structure","p":[16,18],"x":"White's pawns on d4 and e5 against d5 and e6: Black attacks the base with ...c5 and ...f6. White attacks the kingside.","do":"As Black, put pressure on d4 with ...Nc6, ...Qb6 and ...Nf5.","dont":"Don't leave your light bishop locked in for the whole game."},{"id":"mid-70","part":"mid","n":70,"m":"Pawn structures","t":"The Sicilian d5 square","p":[12,18],"x":"In many Sicilians Black's pawn on e5 or e6 leaves the d5 square weak. A white knight on d5 is a monster. Black aims for ...d5 or trades the knight.","do":"As White, fight for d5. As Black, prepare ...d5 or swap the d5 knight.","dont":"Don't let a knight sit on d5 unchallenged."},{"id":"mid-71","part":"mid","n":71,"m":"Pawn structures","t":"The King's Indian structure","p":[16],"x":"With the centre locked (White d5 against Black e5), White attacks on the queenside (c5) and Black on the kingside (...f5 and ...g5). It's a race.","do":"Go to your own side quickly.","dont":"Don't defend the other wing first unless forced.","pr":"game:botvinnik-tal-1960"},{"id":"mid-72","part":"mid","n":72,"m":"Pawn structures","t":"Space advantage","p":[16],"x":"More space means more squares for your pieces. The cramped side should trade pieces, and the side with space should avoid trades.","do":"With more space, keep pieces on and slowly expand.","dont":"Don't let the cramped side free itself with exchanges.","pr":"game:karpov-unzicker-1974"},{"id":"mid-73","part":"mid","n":73,"m":"Pawn structures","t":"Pawn majorities","p":[50],"x":"Three pawns against two on one wing can make a passed pawn. Advance the pawn with no enemy pawn in front (the candidate) first.","do":"Lead with the candidate pawn.","dont":"Don't push the pawn that has an enemy pawn in front of it first.","pr":"theme:advancedPawn"},{"id":"mid-74","part":"mid","n":74,"m":"Pawn structures","t":"Pawn breaks","p":[16,22],"x":"A pawn break opens the position: ...c5, ...f5, b4-b5. Timing is everything. Prepare it with pieces, then strike.","do":"Bring pieces to support the break before playing it.","dont":"Don't break when the opened lines help your opponent more."},{"id":"mid-75","part":"mid","n":75,"m":"Pawn structures","t":"Passed pawns in the middlegame","p":[42],"x":"A protected passed pawn ties down an enemy piece forever. In the middlegame it's a long-term advantage that grows as pieces come off.","do":"Blockade enemy passed pawns with a knight.","dont":"Don't let a passed pawn roll without a blockader.","pr":"theme:advancedPawn"},{"id":"mid-76","part":"mid","n":76,"m":"Pawn structures","t":"Holes and weak squares","p":[12,18],"x":"A square that no pawn can attack is a hole. Your pieces, especially knights, love holes in the enemy camp.","do":"Find squares his pawns can no longer cover, and send a knight there.","dont":"Don't create holes with careless pawn moves."},{"id":"mid-77","part":"mid","n":77,"m":"Pawn structures","t":"Open and half-open files","p":[14],"x":"A file without pawns is open. A file with only enemy pawns is half-open for you. Rooks belong there.","do":"Double rooks on the open file you control.","dont":"Don't let him take the only open file unopposed."},{"id":"mid-78","part":"mid","n":78,"m":"Pawn structures","t":"Open vs closed positions","p":[13,48],"x":"Open positions favour bishops and quick development. Closed positions favour knights and slow manoeuvring.","do":"Steer the position to suit your pieces.","dont":"Don't open the position if he has the bishop pair and you have knights."},{"id":"mid-79","part":"mid","n":79,"m":"Pawn structures","t":"Pawn moves are permanent","p":[8],"x":"Pawns never move backwards. Before every pawn move, ask what squares it weakens for the rest of the game.","do":"Count the squares a pawn move leaves behind.","dont":"Don't play h3, a3 or g4 'just in case'. 32% of your mistakes were pawn moves."},{"id":"mid-80","part":"mid","n":80,"m":"Pawn structures","t":"Centre first, wings later","p":[1,22],"x":"Control or close the centre before attacking on a wing. A flank attack with an open centre gets punished by a central counter-strike.","do":"Stabilise the centre, then attack.","dont":"Don't launch a wing attack with an unstable centre."},{"id":"mid-81","part":"mid","n":81,"m":"Pieces","t":"Good and bad bishops","p":[13],"x":"A bishop whose own pawns sit on its colour is 'bad'. Put your pawns on the opposite colour to your bishop, or trade the bad bishop.","do":"Trade your bad bishop or get it outside the pawn chain.","dont":"Don't fix all your pawns on your bishop's colour.","pr":"game:fischer-petrosian-1971"},{"id":"mid-82","part":"mid","n":82,"m":"Pieces","t":"The bishop pair","p":[13],"x":"Two bishops together control both colours and are strong in open positions. Open the position, and avoid trading one of them cheaply.","do":"With the bishop pair, open the position.","dont":"Don't give up a bishop for a knight without a reason when you have the pair."},{"id":"mid-83","part":"mid","n":83,"m":"Pieces","t":"Knight outposts","p":[12],"x":"A knight on a protected square in enemy territory, where no pawn can attack it, is often stronger than a rook.","do":"Find a protected outpost and plan the knight's route there.","dont":"Don't leave your knight on the rim. A knight on the rim is dim.","pr":"game:karpov-kasparov-1985"},{"id":"mid-84","part":"mid","n":84,"m":"Pieces","t":"Knight vs bishop in the middlegame","p":[12,13,48],"x":"Knights love closed positions and outposts. Bishops love open lines. Trade the minor piece that is worse in the coming structure.","do":"Before trading, picture the structure that will result.","dont":"Don't automatically 'win the bishop pair' if the position will stay closed."},{"id":"mid-85","part":"mid","n":85,"m":"Pieces","t":"Rook placement","p":[14],"x":"Rooks go to open files, then the seventh rank. Behind passed pawns (yours or his) is also good.","do":"Connect rooks, then take the open file.","dont":"Don't leave a rook on a closed file for the whole game."},{"id":"mid-86","part":"mid","n":86,"m":"Pieces","t":"Doubling rooks","p":[14,15],"x":"Two rooks on the same file are twice as strong. The front one can invade, supported by the one behind.","do":"Double on the open file before invading.","dont":"Don't invade with one rook when he can trade it off."},{"id":"mid-87","part":"mid","n":87,"m":"Pieces","t":"Rooks on the seventh","p":[15],"x":"A rook on the seventh attacks unmoved pawns and confines the king. Two rooks there are often decisive.","do":"Head for the seventh rank once you own an open file.","dont":"Don't let his rook reach your second rank.","pr":"game:fischer-petrosian-1971"},{"id":"mid-88","part":"mid","n":88,"m":"Pieces","t":"The centralised queen and its dangers","p":[4],"x":"A queen in the centre is powerful but can be chased. Centralise her when she can't be harassed with tempo.","do":"Put the queen where minor pieces can't attack her.","dont":"Don't bring the queen out early. Your 2026 losses with early Qxd4 show why."},{"id":"mid-89","part":"mid","n":89,"m":"Pieces","t":"The positional exchange sacrifice","p":[37],"x":"Giving a rook for a minor piece can be good if it wrecks his structure or wins a key square. Petrosian and Kasparov often did it.","do":"Consider ...Rxc3 against a structure with doubled c-pawns.","dont":"Don't sacrifice the exchange without lasting compensation.","pr":"game:fischer-spassky-1972"},{"id":"mid-90","part":"mid","n":90,"m":"Pieces","t":"Piece for three pawns, and other imbalances","p":[37,42],"x":"A minor piece is roughly three pawns. In the endgame, three connected passed pawns can beat a piece. In the middlegame, the piece usually wins.","do":"Count active pieces, not just material points.","dont":"Don't trade into an endgame with lots of passed pawns against your piece."},{"id":"mid-91","part":"mid","n":91,"m":"Pieces","t":"Queen vs two rooks","p":[17],"x":"Two rooks are usually a match for a queen when they can protect each other. The queen does better against loose pieces.","do":"With two rooks, double them and keep them defended.","dont":"Don't let your rooks become loose targets for queen forks."},{"id":"mid-92","part":"mid","n":92,"m":"Pieces","t":"Coordination","p":[11,17],"x":"Pieces work best together, defending each other and aimed at the same target. Uncoordinated pieces fall to double attacks.","do":"Arrange pieces so they protect each other.","dont":"Don't have pieces on opposite sides of the board with no shared goal."},{"id":"mid-93","part":"mid","n":93,"m":"Pieces","t":"Which pieces to trade","p":[20],"x":"Trade your bad pieces for his good ones. Trade attackers when defending and defenders when attacking. When ahead, trade pieces.","do":"Before trading, ask whose piece is better.","dont":"Don't trade just because you can."},{"id":"mid-94","part":"mid","n":94,"m":"Pieces","t":"When to trade queens","p":[20,39],"x":"Trade queens when you're ahead, when his king is safer than yours, or when the endgame structure favours you.","do":"Offer queen trades when you're up material.","dont":"Don't trade queens when you have the attack.","pr":"game:fischer-petrosian-1971"},{"id":"mid-95","part":"mid","n":95,"m":"Pieces","t":"Knights on the rim","p":[11,12],"x":"A knight on a or h controls half as many squares. Bring it back to the centre unless it has a specific job (like Na5xc4).","do":"Reroute rim knights towards the centre.","dont":"Don't go to the rim for a single threat that is easily parried."},{"id":"mid-96","part":"mid","n":96,"m":"Planning","t":"Make a plan from the imbalances","p":[9,21],"x":"Compare material, king safety, pawn structure, piece activity, space and the open files. Your plan uses your pluses and his minuses.","do":"At each quiet moment, list the imbalances.","dont":"Don't play moves without a reason."},{"id":"mid-97","part":"mid","n":97,"m":"Planning","t":"Convert an advantage: Fischer mode","p":[39,20],"x":"When winning, simplify: trade pieces, remove counterplay, keep the king safe, and double-check every enemy check and capture.","do":"Make the boring move that keeps everything safe.","dont":"Don't look for the most beautiful win.","pr":"game:fischer-taimanov-1971"},{"id":"mid-98","part":"mid","n":98,"m":"Planning","t":"Play against two weaknesses","p":[19],"x":"Fix one weakness, then attack a second one on the other side. The defender can't cover both.","do":"Switch wings to stretch his defence.","dont":"Don't attack only one fixed weakness that he defends easily."},{"id":"mid-99","part":"mid","n":99,"m":"Planning","t":"The transition to the endgame","p":[20,41],"x":"The most important decisions happen when trading into an endgame. Check whether the resulting endgame is won before simplifying.","do":"Picture the endgame before the last trades.","dont":"Don't trade into a pawn ending without counting."},{"id":"mid-100","part":"mid","n":100,"m":"Planning","t":"Practical decisions with the clock","p":[38,40],"x":"In rapid, a good practical move found in time beats a perfect move found too late. Take your time at critical moments and play simple, safe moves elsewhere.","do":"Spend time on the moves that change the game.","dont":"Don't use 5 minutes on an obvious recapture."},{"id":"end-1","part":"end","n":1,"m":"Basic checkmates","t":"King and queen vs king","p":[41],"x":"Use the queen to shrink the king's box, a knight's move away from it, then bring your own king close and mate on the edge. It takes about ten moves from any position.","do":"Keep the queen a knight's jump from the enemy king while your king walks over.","dont":"Don't give endless checks. Checks don't shrink the box.","fen":"8/8/8/4k3/8/8/8/4K2Q w - - 0 1","cap":"White mates in about 10. Box the king in, then bring your king."},{"id":"end-2","part":"end","n":2,"m":"Basic checkmates","t":"King and rook vs king","p":[41],"x":"The rook cuts the king off along a rank or file. Your king takes the opposition, and the rook checks to push the enemy king back one line at a time. Mate comes on the edge.","do":"Use 'waiting' rook moves along the line to force the king to give way.","dont":"Don't put the rook next to the enemy king without your king's protection.","fen":"8/8/8/4k3/8/8/8/R3K3 w - - 0 1","cap":"Rook cuts, king opposes, rook checks."},{"id":"end-3","part":"end","n":3,"m":"Basic checkmates","t":"Two rooks: the ladder mate","p":[41],"x":"Two rooks mate without the king: they take turns checking on neighbouring ranks, pushing the king to the edge like climbing a ladder.","do":"Keep the rooks far from the enemy king so it can't attack them.","dont":"Don't put the rooks on the same rank where the king can approach.","fen":"8/8/8/4k3/8/8/8/RR2K3 w - - 0 1","cap":"Alternate checks on rank after rank."},{"id":"end-4","part":"end","n":4,"m":"Basic checkmates","t":"Two bishops vs king","p":[41],"x":"Two bishops side by side form a wall the king can't cross. Drive it into a corner (any corner) with king support, then mate.","do":"Keep the bishops next to each other, like a fence.","dont":"Don't stalemate: always leave the king one move.","fen":"8/8/8/4k3/8/8/8/2B1KB2 w - - 0 1","cap":"The bishops make a diagonal fence."},{"id":"end-5","part":"end","n":5,"m":"Basic checkmates","t":"Bishop and knight vs king","p":[41],"x":"The hardest basic mate (up to 33 moves). The king must be driven to a corner of the bishop's colour, using the 'W' manoeuvre with the knight. Know that it's a win, and learn it later.","do":"Drive the king to a corner the bishop controls.","dont":"Don't panic about the 50-move rule. Learn the W manoeuvre once.","fen":"8/8/8/4k3/8/8/8/1N2KB2 w - - 0 1","cap":"A win, but technical: corner of the bishop's colour."},{"id":"end-6","part":"end","n":6,"m":"Basic checkmates","t":"Two knights can't force mate","p":[47],"x":"King and two knights against a lone king is a draw: the defender always escapes stalemate traps. (With an extra enemy pawn on the board, mate can sometimes be forced.)","do":"If you're defending, happily give up your last pieces for the opponent's pawns to reach K+2N.","dont":"Don't trade into K+2N vs K expecting to win.","fen":"8/8/8/4k3/8/8/8/1N2K1N1 w - - 0 1","cap":"A theoretical draw."},{"id":"end-7","part":"end","n":7,"m":"Basic checkmates","t":"Stalemate: the queen's trap","p":[39],"x":"With a queen against a lone king, it's easy to take away every square without giving check. That's stalemate, a draw. Before every queen move, check that the king still has a move.","do":"Count his legal moves before each queen move.","dont":"Don't play Qb6 here with the king on a8: that's stalemate.","fen":"k7/8/8/1Q6/8/8/8/4K3 w - - 0 1","cap":"Avoid Qb6?? stalemate. Bring the king."},{"id":"end-8","part":"end","n":8,"m":"Basic checkmates","t":"Mating with the king's help","p":[41],"x":"Except for the two rooks, every basic mate needs your king. A common mistake is to chase with the piece while your king stays home.","do":"Walk your king towards the enemy king early.","dont":"Don't make piece moves that don't shrink the box."},{"id":"end-9","part":"end","n":9,"m":"Basic checkmates","t":"The 50-move rule","p":[47],"x":"If 50 moves pass with no capture and no pawn move, the game is a draw on claim (75 is automatic online). Learn basic mates well enough to finish in time.","do":"Practise basic mates against the engine until they're fast.","dont":"Don't wander with the pieces in a winning ending."},{"id":"end-10","part":"end","n":10,"m":"Basic checkmates","t":"Checkmate patterns on the edge","p":[28],"x":"Almost all endgame mates happen on the edge or in a corner, where the king has the fewest squares. Your plan is always: edge first, then mate.","do":"Push the king to the edge before looking for mate.","dont":"Don't look for mate in the middle of the board.","pr":"theme:mateIn2"},{"id":"end-11","part":"end","n":11,"m":"King and pawn endings","t":"The king is a fighting piece","p":[41],"x":"Once the queens are off, march your king to the centre. In pawn endings the more active king usually wins.","do":"Centralise the king at the start of every pawn ending.","dont":"Don't leave the king on g1 while the pawns decide the game.","pr":"theme:pawnEndgame"},{"id":"end-12","part":"end","n":12,"m":"King and pawn endings","t":"Opposition","p":[44],"x":"When kings face each other with one square between them, the side that must move has to give way. Having the opposition means it's your opponent's turn in that position.","do":"Learn to take the opposition: put your king opposite his with him to move.","dont":"Don't rush the pawn. King first, then pawn.","pr":"theme:pawnEndgame","fen":"8/8/8/3k4/8/3K4/3P4/8 b - - 0 1","cap":"Black to move must give way: White wins. With White to move it is a draw."},{"id":"end-13","part":"end","n":13,"m":"King and pawn endings","t":"King in front of the pawn","p":[44,41],"x":"With king and pawn against king, put your king in front of the pawn, not behind it. Key squares two ranks in front of the pawn decide everything.","do":"Get your king to one of the key squares ahead of the pawn.","dont":"Don't push the pawn ahead of your king.","pr":"theme:pawnEndgame"},{"id":"end-14","part":"end","n":14,"m":"King and pawn endings","t":"The king on the sixth rank wins","p":[44],"x":"If your king stands on the 6th rank directly in front of your (non-rook) pawn, you win whoever is to move. It's an easy target to remember.","do":"Aim for this position when you have K+P vs K.","dont":"Don't swap into K+P vs K unless your king can get in front.","pr":"theme:pawnEndgame","fen":"4k3/8/4K3/4P3/8/8/8/8 w - - 0 1","cap":"White wins with either side to move."},{"id":"end-15","part":"end","n":15,"m":"King and pawn endings","t":"The rook pawn exception","p":[47],"x":"With an a- or h-pawn, the defending king in the corner can't be driven out. It's a draw even with the attacking king close.","do":"If you're defending against a rook pawn, run to the corner.","dont":"Don't count on winning K + rook pawn vs K.","pr":"theme:pawnEndgame","fen":"7k/8/7K/7P/8/8/8/8 w - - 0 1","cap":"A draw: Black's king never leaves the corner."},{"id":"end-16","part":"end","n":16,"m":"King and pawn endings","t":"The rule of the square","p":[45],"x":"Draw a square from the pawn to its queening square. If the defending king can step into it, the pawn is caught. It saves you calculating.","do":"Use the square before every pawn race.","dont":"Don't forget that a pawn on its starting square can jump two squares.","pr":"theme:pawnEndgame","fen":"8/8/8/p7/8/8/6K1/8 w - - 0 1","cap":"Kg2 is one step outside the square: the pawn queens."},{"id":"end-17","part":"end","n":17,"m":"King and pawn endings","t":"Distant opposition","p":[44],"x":"Kings on the same file with an odd number of squares between them: the side not to move has the 'distant' opposition and can turn it into the close opposition.","do":"Count the squares between the kings.","dont":"Don't step closer if it hands him the opposition."},{"id":"end-18","part":"end","n":18,"m":"King and pawn endings","t":"Triangulation","p":[44],"x":"Your king makes three moves in a triangle to come back to the same square, while his king can only shuffle two. It 'loses' a move so he has to give way.","do":"Use triangulation when you need to hand him the move.","dont":"Don't think pawn endings are only about counting. Tempo tricks decide them."},{"id":"end-19","part":"end","n":19,"m":"King and pawn endings","t":"Outside passed pawn","p":[50,42],"x":"A passed pawn far from the other pawns (an outside passer) pulls the enemy king away. Your king then eats the pawns on the other side.","do":"Create the passed pawn on the wing far from the kings.","dont":"Don't trade it off early. Its job is to be a decoy.","pr":"theme:pawnEndgame","fen":"8/5kpp/8/P7/8/8/5KPP/8 w - - 0 1","cap":"The a-pawn drags the black king away."},{"id":"end-20","part":"end","n":20,"m":"King and pawn endings","t":"Protected passed pawn","p":[42],"x":"A passed pawn defended by another pawn can't be taken by the king. It often wins pawn endings by itself.","do":"Value a protected passer highly when trading down.","dont":"Don't let him get one cheaply."},{"id":"end-21","part":"end","n":21,"m":"King and pawn endings","t":"Connected passed pawns","p":[42],"x":"Two passed pawns side by side protect each other and advance together. A king alone can't stop them once they reach the 6th.","do":"Push them side by side, or with one guarding the other.","dont":"Don't push one too far ahead where it can be picked off.","pr":"theme:advancedPawn"},{"id":"end-22","part":"end","n":22,"m":"King and pawn endings","t":"The pawn breakthrough","p":[42],"x":"Three pawns against three facing each other can break through with a sacrifice: with White pawns a5, b5, c5 against a7, b7, c7, 1.b6! wins.","do":"Look for breakthrough sacrifices when your pawns are further advanced.","dont":"Don't allow his pawns to reach your 3rd rank opposite yours.","pr":"theme:advancedPawn","fen":"7k/ppp5/8/PPP5/8/8/8/7K w - - 0 1","cap":"1.b6! and one pawn queens."},{"id":"end-23","part":"end","n":23,"m":"King and pawn endings","t":"Shouldering","p":[41],"x":"Use your king to block the enemy king from reaching the action, like a shoulder barge. It wins tempi in races.","do":"Walk your king between his king and the pawns.","dont":"Don't walk your king around his. Walk in front of it."},{"id":"end-24","part":"end","n":24,"m":"King and pawn endings","t":"Counting the race","p":[45],"x":"In a pawn race, count the moves for each side to queen, including checks the new queen might give. One tempo decides it.","do":"Count moves exactly before you start a race.","dont":"Don't race if his pawn queens with check.","pr":"theme:pawnEndgame"},{"id":"end-25","part":"end","n":25,"m":"King and pawn endings","t":"Queen vs pawn on the seventh","p":[47],"x":"A queen beats a b-, d-, e- or g-pawn on the 7th by checks that force the king in front of the pawn, then the king approaches. Against an a- or c-pawn (or f- and h-) the defender often draws by stalemate tricks.","do":"Know which pawns are draws: rook and bishop pawns.","dont":"Don't assume a queen always beats a pawn.","fen":"8/8/8/8/8/8/3pk3/K6Q w - - 0 1","cap":"A centre pawn: White wins."},{"id":"end-26","part":"end","n":26,"m":"King and pawn endings","t":"Spare pawn moves","p":[44],"x":"In pawn endings, spare tempi (pawn moves you can make without harm) decide who has to give way. Count them.","do":"Keep spare pawn moves in reserve.","dont":"Don't waste your spare moves early."},{"id":"end-27","part":"end","n":27,"m":"King and pawn endings","t":"Pawn endings are calculable","p":[33],"x":"Pawn endings are pure calculation. Before trading the last pieces, calculate the pawn ending to the end. It's either won, drawn or lost.","do":"Before swapping into a pawn ending, count every move.","dont":"Don't trade into a pawn ending 'because it looks equal'.","pr":"theme:pawnEndgame"},{"id":"end-28","part":"end","n":28,"m":"King and pawn endings","t":"Doubled and isolated pawns in pawn endings","p":[18],"x":"Weak pawns are much weaker in pawn endings, where the king can attack them.","do":"Aim for pawn endings when his structure is broken.","dont":"Don't trade into a pawn ending with your own doubled pawns."},{"id":"end-29","part":"end","n":29,"m":"King and pawn endings","t":"The active king wins pawns","p":[41],"x":"In pawn endings the king that reaches the enemy pawns first usually wins: it eats them while the other king is busy.","do":"Race your king to his weak pawns.","dont":"Don't waste king moves defending what can't be attacked.","pr":"theme:pawnEndgame"},{"id":"end-30","part":"end","n":30,"m":"King and pawn endings","t":"Zugzwang","p":[44],"x":"Zugzwang means any move worsens your position. In endgames it's a weapon. Put your opponent in a position where he must move and every move loses.","do":"Look for positions where he must give way.","dont":"Don't hurry when a waiting move puts him in zugzwang."},{"id":"end-31","part":"end","n":31,"m":"King and pawn endings","t":"Keep pawns on both wings when you're better","p":[19],"x":"With pawns on both wings, the defender has two things to watch. With all pawns on one side, draws are much more likely.","do":"When ahead, keep pawns on both sides.","dont":"Don't trade the pawns on the side where your king can't reach."},{"id":"end-32","part":"end","n":32,"m":"Rook endings","t":"Rook endings are the most common","p":[46],"x":"About half of all endgames are rook endings. They are drawish when material is close, so technique and activity decide.","do":"Study rook endings first among all endgames.","dont":"Don't assume one extra pawn in a rook ending is an easy win.","pr":"theme:rookEndgame"},{"id":"end-33","part":"end","n":33,"m":"Rook endings","t":"Activity beats material","p":[46],"x":"An active rook (attacking pawns, cutting the king) is often worth a pawn. Capablanca and Rubinstein gave pawns to activate their rooks.","do":"Keep your rook active even at the cost of a pawn.","dont":"Don't let your rook become a passive defender of a pawn.","pr":"game:capablanca-tartakower-1924"},{"id":"end-34","part":"end","n":34,"m":"Rook endings","t":"The Lucena position: build a bridge","p":[47],"x":"King on the queening square, pawn on the 7th, the enemy king cut off by a file: you win by 'building a bridge'. Rook to the 4th rank, king walks out, the rook blocks the checks.","do":"Remember: rook check to push his king away, rook to the 4th, king out.","dont":"Don't walk out with the king before the rook can block checks.","pr":"theme:rookEndgame","fen":"1K6/1P1k4/8/8/8/8/r7/2R5 w - - 0 1","cap":"1.Rd1+, then Rd4 and the bridge."},{"id":"end-35","part":"end","n":35,"m":"Rook endings","t":"The Philidor position: third-rank defence","p":[47],"x":"The defending rook stays on its third rank, stopping the enemy king. When the pawn advances to that rank, the rook drops to the back and checks from behind. It's a draw.","do":"Keep the rook on your third rank until the pawn moves to it.","dont":"Don't put the rook on the back rank too early.","pr":"theme:rookEndgame","fen":"4k3/8/r7/4PK2/8/8/8/7R b - - 0 1","cap":"Black holds with ...Ra6 (third rank), then checks from behind."},{"id":"end-36","part":"end","n":36,"m":"Rook endings","t":"Cut the king off","p":[46],"x":"Use your rook to cut the defending king off from the pawn along a file or rank. One file of cut-off is often the difference between a win and a draw.","do":"Put your rook on the file between his king and your pawn.","dont":"Don't check aimlessly. Cut off.","pr":"theme:rookEndgame"},{"id":"end-37","part":"end","n":37,"m":"Rook endings","t":"Rook behind the passed pawn","p":[43],"x":"Rooks belong behind passed pawns, yours and his. Behind your pawn, the rook gains scope as the pawn advances. Behind his, it attacks it.","do":"Put your rook behind passed pawns.","dont":"Don't put your rook in front of your own passed pawn.","pr":"theme:rookEndgame"},{"id":"end-38","part":"end","n":38,"m":"Rook endings","t":"The short side defence","p":[47],"x":"When defending rook and pawn vs rook, put your king on the short side of the pawn (fewer files) and your rook on the long side, to check from a distance.","do":"Check from far away. Your rook needs distance.","dont":"Don't check from close range where his king can attack your rook.","pr":"theme:rookEndgame"},{"id":"end-39","part":"end","n":39,"m":"Rook endings","t":"Checking distance","p":[46],"x":"A rook needs at least three files between itself and the enemy king to give useful checks. Too close, and the king attacks the rook.","do":"Keep your checking rook far away.","dont":"Don't check from the next file."},{"id":"end-40","part":"end","n":40,"m":"Rook endings","t":"Rook vs pawn","p":[41,43],"x":"A rook usually beats a pawn if its king is close enough. Otherwise the pawn, supported by its king, can draw. Cut the king off and bring your own.","do":"Cut off the enemy king first, then bring your king.","dont":"Don't go pawn-hunting with your rook alone."},{"id":"end-41","part":"end","n":41,"m":"Rook endings","t":"Rook vs two connected pawns","p":[42],"x":"Two connected passed pawns on the 6th beat a rook if the king can't help. Count carefully.","do":"If you have the pawns, push them together.","dont":"Don't let them both reach the 6th if you have the rook."},{"id":"end-42","part":"end","n":42,"m":"Rook endings","t":"Four against three on one side","p":[47],"x":"An extra pawn with all pawns on the same wing in a rook ending is usually a draw with good defence.","do":"If defending, keep the rook active and the king near the pawns.","dont":"Don't trade the other-wing pawns if you're the stronger side.","pr":"theme:rookEndgame"},{"id":"end-43","part":"end","n":43,"m":"Rook endings","t":"The king is the best defender","p":[41],"x":"In rook endings, put the defending king in front of the pawn. With the king on the queening square, most positions are drawn.","do":"Run your king to the queening square.","dont":"Don't keep the king far away while the rook defends alone.","pr":"theme:rookEndgame"},{"id":"end-44","part":"end","n":44,"m":"Rook endings","t":"Rook on the seventh in the endgame","p":[15],"x":"A rook on the 7th attacks pawns and confines the king to the back rank. It's often decisive even without extra material.","do":"Get your rook to the 7th as soon as it is safe.","dont":"Don't let his rook reach your 2nd rank."},{"id":"end-45","part":"end","n":45,"m":"Rook endings","t":"Passive rook = lost rook ending","p":[46],"x":"A rook tied to defending a pawn from the side or front is passive, and passive rook endings usually lose.","do":"Counterattack his pawns rather than defend yours.","dont":"Don't let your rook become a pawn's babysitter.","pr":"theme:rookEndgame"},{"id":"end-46","part":"end","n":46,"m":"Rook endings","t":"Trade into a won pawn ending","p":[20,33],"x":"Sometimes the winning plan is to trade rooks. If the resulting pawn ending is won, offer the trade. Calculate it first.","do":"Calculate the pawn ending before offering the rook trade.","dont":"Don't avoid trades out of habit.","pr":"theme:pawnEndgame"},{"id":"end-47","part":"end","n":47,"m":"Rook endings","t":"Don't trade into a lost pawn ending","p":[33],"x":"The defender's mistake: trading rooks into a lost pawn ending. Keep the rooks on when the pawn ending is bad.","do":"When defending, keep rooks on unless the pawn ending is a draw.","dont":"Don't trade rooks to 'relieve the pressure'."},{"id":"end-48","part":"end","n":48,"m":"Rook endings","t":"Two rooks endings","p":[46],"x":"With two rooks each, the side that doubles on the 7th or opens a file first usually wins. Coordination matters more than material.","do":"Double rooks on the 7th.","dont":"Don't leave your rooks uncoordinated."},{"id":"end-49","part":"end","n":49,"m":"Rook endings","t":"Rook and passed pawn vs rook: push or defend?","p":[42,46],"x":"With an extra passed pawn, use the rook to support it from behind and bring the king. Push when it's safe, not before.","do":"Rook behind, king in front, then push.","dont":"Don't push the pawn so far it gets cut off and lost.","pr":"theme:rookEndgame"},{"id":"end-50","part":"end","n":50,"m":"Rook endings","t":"The Vancura idea (rook pawn defence)","p":[47],"x":"Against a rook pawn on the 6th with the rook in front of it, the defending rook attacks the pawn from the side, from the 6th rank, and draws.","do":"Defend rook-pawn endings by attacking the pawn from the side.","dont":"Don't defend passively from behind with the king far away."},{"id":"end-51","part":"end","n":51,"m":"Rook endings (more)","t":"Rook and pawn: the king cut off by one file","p":[46,47],"x":"With your king near your pawn and his king cut off by just one file, you can often still win if the pawn is far advanced. With two files of cut-off it's usually a win.","do":"Aim to cut the king off by two files.","dont":"Don't let his king cross to the pawn's file.","pr":"theme:rookEndgame"},{"id":"end-52","part":"end","n":52,"m":"Rook endings (more)","t":"Defending with the rook in front of the pawn","p":[47],"x":"When your rook stands in front of a passed pawn (not behind), the attacker's rook has to stay behind it, and his king becomes the key. Keep your king close.","do":"If your rook is in front, bring your king towards the pawn.","dont":"Don't let your king wander away from the passed pawn."},{"id":"end-53","part":"end","n":53,"m":"Rook endings (more)","t":"Rook endings with pawns on both wings","p":[19,46],"x":"Pawns on both wings favour the active side. Create a passed pawn on one wing while your king supports the other.","do":"Stretch the defence across the board.","dont":"Don't trade all the pawns on one wing if you're pressing.","pr":"theme:rookEndgame"},{"id":"end-54","part":"end","n":54,"m":"Rook endings (more)","t":"Checking from behind","p":[43,47],"x":"Checks from behind are the safest, because the attacking king can't approach the rook. In Philidor and Vancura defences, the rook goes behind.","do":"When the pawn advances, check from behind or far away.","dont":"Don't check from in front, where the king hides behind the pawn."},{"id":"end-55","part":"end","n":55,"m":"Rook endings (more)","t":"Practical rook ending rules","p":[46,43,41],"x":"Rook active, king active, rook behind passers, cut the king off, and check from a distance. Most rook endings are decided by these five rules.","do":"Run through the five rules at every move in a rook ending.","dont":"Don't think 'a pawn up means winning' in rook endings.","pr":"theme:rookEndgame"},{"id":"end-56","part":"end","n":56,"m":"Minor piece endings","t":"Bishop vs knight: pawns on both wings","p":[48],"x":"With pawns on both wings, the long-range bishop is usually better. It can watch both sides, while the knight can only be on one.","do":"Keep pawns on both wings if you have the bishop.","dont":"Don't fix your pawns on your bishop's colour.","pr":"game:fischer-taimanov-1971"},{"id":"end-57","part":"end","n":57,"m":"Minor piece endings","t":"Bishop vs knight: closed, one-wing positions","p":[48],"x":"With all pawns on one wing, or a blocked structure, the knight can be equal or better. It reaches every square colour.","do":"With the knight, aim for blocked pawns and one-wing play.","dont":"Don't open lines for the bishop."},{"id":"end-58","part":"end","n":58,"m":"Minor piece endings","t":"The good knight vs the bad bishop","p":[13,48],"x":"If his bishop is blocked by its own pawns, a knight on a strong square dominates it. The textbook favourable ending.","do":"Fix his pawns on his bishop's colour, then bring the knight to an outpost.","dont":"Don't free his bad bishop by opening the position."},{"id":"end-59","part":"end","n":59,"m":"Minor piece endings","t":"Same-coloured bishops","p":[13],"x":"With bishops of the same colour, the side whose pawns are on the other colour has the good bishop, and good bishops win pawns.","do":"Put your pawns on the opposite colour of your bishop.","dont":"Don't put your pawns where his bishop can attack them."},{"id":"end-60","part":"end","n":60,"m":"Minor piece endings","t":"Opposite-coloured bishops are drawish","p":[47],"x":"With bishops on opposite colours, even two extra pawns may not win, because the defender's bishop blockades on its colour. A great drawing resource.","do":"If you're defending, aim for opposite-coloured bishops.","dont":"Don't trade into them when you're the stronger side."},{"id":"end-61","part":"end","n":61,"m":"Minor piece endings","t":"Winning with opposite-coloured bishops","p":[19],"x":"They win when you have two passed pawns far apart (at least three files): the defending bishop can't stop both.","do":"Create passed pawns on different wings.","dont":"Don't make connected passers that one bishop can stop on a single diagonal."},{"id":"end-62","part":"end","n":62,"m":"Minor piece endings","t":"The wrong bishop and the rook pawn","p":[47],"x":"King, bishop and rook-pawn win only if the bishop controls the queening square. With the 'wrong' bishop, the defender's king sits in the corner and it's a draw.","do":"As the defender, run your king to the corner.","dont":"Don't trade into K+B+rook pawn if your bishop is the wrong colour.","fen":"7k/8/6KP/8/8/8/3B4/8 w - - 0 1","cap":"The dark bishop controls h8: mate in 3. With a light bishop it's a draw."},{"id":"end-63","part":"end","n":63,"m":"Minor piece endings","t":"Knight endings are like pawn endings","p":[41],"x":"Knight endings behave much like pawn endings: the active king and outside passed pawns decide. Knights are slow to switch wings.","do":"Use the king actively. Make an outside passer.","dont":"Don't count on the knight to stop a far-away pawn."},{"id":"end-64","part":"end","n":64,"m":"Minor piece endings","t":"Knight vs rook pawn","p":[47],"x":"A knight struggles to stop a rook pawn, because it has few squares near the edge. A knight in front of the pawn on the queening square can still hold.","do":"Defend by getting the knight in front of the pawn.","dont":"Don't let the pawn reach the 7th with the knight far away."},{"id":"end-65","part":"end","n":65,"m":"Minor piece endings","t":"Bishop and pawns vs bishop: the blockade","p":[47],"x":"The defending bishop draws if it can block the pawn on a square of its colour that the attacking bishop can't attack.","do":"Find a blockade square of your bishop's colour.","dont":"Don't let the pawn reach squares your bishop can't control."},{"id":"end-66","part":"end","n":66,"m":"Minor piece endings","t":"The bishop dominating the knight","p":[48],"x":"Sometimes a bishop takes every square from a knight, so it can't move. Look for domination when the knight is near the edge.","do":"Take away the knight's squares with your bishop and king.","dont":"Don't put your knight on the edge near his bishop.","pr":"game:fischer-taimanov-1971"},{"id":"end-67","part":"end","n":67,"m":"Minor piece endings","t":"Minor piece vs pawns","p":[42],"x":"A minor piece usually stops one or two pawns but can lose to three connected passers. Count the tempi.","do":"With the pawns, push them together, supported by the king.","dont":"Don't separate connected pawns."},{"id":"end-68","part":"end","n":68,"m":"Minor piece endings","t":"Two bishops in the endgame","p":[13],"x":"The bishop pair is strongest in endgames with pawns on both sides. They restrict knights and support passed pawns.","do":"Keep both bishops when the board is open.","dont":"Don't trade a bishop for a knight in an open endgame without a reason."},{"id":"end-69","part":"end","n":69,"m":"Minor piece endings","t":"Bishop and knight together","p":[11],"x":"Knights and bishops cooperate well. The knight covers the squares the bishop can't. In endgames, centralise both.","do":"Put the knight on squares of the colour your bishop doesn't cover.","dont":"Don't leave the knight far from the action."},{"id":"end-70","part":"end","n":70,"m":"Minor piece endings","t":"Piece and pawns vs piece: create a second weakness","p":[19],"x":"An extra pawn with minor pieces wins when you stretch the defender. Advance on one wing, then switch.","do":"Create a passed pawn, then attack on the other wing.","dont":"Don't push the passer alone into a blockade."},{"id":"end-71","part":"end","n":71,"m":"Minor piece endings","t":"Blockading passed pawns with a knight","p":[12],"x":"The knight is the best blockader. It stands in front of the pawn and still attacks squares around it.","do":"Blockade enemy passers with a knight.","dont":"Don't blockade with the queen or a rook if a minor piece can do it."},{"id":"end-72","part":"end","n":72,"m":"Minor piece endings","t":"King activity in minor piece endings","p":[41],"x":"In minor piece endings the king is often the decisive piece: it attacks pawns the pieces can't reach.","do":"March the king towards his pawns.","dont":"Don't keep the king passive while the pieces dance."},{"id":"end-73","part":"end","n":73,"m":"Minor piece endings","t":"Pawns on the right colour","p":[13,18],"x":"In bishop endings, put your pawns on the opposite colour to your bishop (so it can defend the other squares), and attack his pawns on your bishop's colour.","do":"Fix his pawns on your bishop's colour.","dont":"Don't put your pawns where his bishop can attack them."},{"id":"end-74","part":"end","n":74,"m":"Minor piece endings","t":"Minor piece vs rook endings","p":[46],"x":"A rook beats a minor piece when pawns are on the board, but without pawns rook vs bishop or rook vs knight is usually a draw.","do":"When down the exchange in an endgame, aim to trade all pawns.","dont":"Don't trade the pawns when you have the extra exchange."},{"id":"end-75","part":"end","n":75,"m":"Minor piece endings","t":"Bishop vs pawns: stop them on one diagonal","p":[48],"x":"A bishop stops connected pawns well when it can attack the squares in front of both from one diagonal.","do":"Find the diagonal that covers both pawns' path.","dont":"Don't chase the pawns with the king when the bishop alone can hold."},{"id":"end-76","part":"end","n":76,"m":"Minor piece endings","t":"Knight forks in the endgame","p":[29],"x":"With fewer pieces, forks become more powerful: king and pawn forks, and king and piece forks. Watch your king's position.","do":"Look for knight forks on the king and an undefended piece.","dont":"Don't put your king a knight's move from your loose pieces.","pr":"theme:fork"},{"id":"end-77","part":"end","n":77,"m":"Queen and mixed endings","t":"Queen endings: centralise and check","p":[41],"x":"In queen endings, a centralised queen controls everything. Checks are the main defensive weapon. Perpetual check is always lurking.","do":"Put your queen in the centre.","dont":"Don't let your king be open to endless checks."},{"id":"end-78","part":"end","n":78,"m":"Queen and mixed endings","t":"Queen and passed pawn vs queen","p":[42],"x":"The passed pawn wins if your king can hide from checks near the pawn. Use your queen to block checks with a counter-check.","do":"Use your king as a shelter for the pawn.","dont":"Don't push the pawn when your king is exposed."},{"id":"end-79","part":"end","n":79,"m":"Queen and mixed endings","t":"Queen vs rook","p":[47],"x":"Queen vs rook (no pawns) is a win with technique: push the king to the edge and win the rook by forks or zugzwang. It takes practice.","do":"Drive his king and rook apart.","dont":"Don't allow a rook check that leads to stalemate or a skewer."},{"id":"end-80","part":"end","n":80,"m":"Queen and mixed endings","t":"Queen vs rook and pawn: fortresses","p":[47],"x":"A rook and pawn sometimes build a fortress against a queen: the rook shuttles while the pawn protects it. Know that some positions can't be won.","do":"As the defender, look for a rook-and-pawn fortress.","dont":"Don't assume a queen always wins."},{"id":"end-81","part":"end","n":81,"m":"Queen and mixed endings","t":"Rook vs bishop: the right corner","p":[47],"x":"Rook vs bishop with no pawns is a draw if the defending king goes to the corner the bishop does NOT control.","do":"Defend in the corner of the opposite colour to your bishop.","dont":"Don't run to the bishop's colour corner."},{"id":"end-82","part":"end","n":82,"m":"Queen and mixed endings","t":"Rook vs knight","p":[47],"x":"Rook vs knight with no pawns is usually a draw if the knight stays close to its king. A knight cut off from its king can be lost.","do":"Keep the knight next to your king.","dont":"Don't let the knight get separated from the king."},{"id":"end-83","part":"end","n":83,"m":"Queen and mixed endings","t":"Rook and bishop vs rook","p":[47],"x":"Rook and bishop vs rook is a theoretical draw, but it's very hard to defend over the board. The defender must know the 'second rank defence'.","do":"If defending, keep your rook active on your second rank and your king central.","dont":"Don't think it's easy just because it's a draw in theory."},{"id":"end-84","part":"end","n":84,"m":"Queen and mixed endings","t":"Queen vs two rooks in the endgame","p":[17],"x":"Two rooks working together match a queen. They win loose pawns and give mate threats. The queen wins against uncoordinated rooks.","do":"Keep your rooks defending each other.","dont":"Don't leave a rook loose for a queen fork."},{"id":"end-85","part":"end","n":85,"m":"Queen and mixed endings","t":"The exchange up","p":[46],"x":"A rook against a minor piece wins most endgames with pawns. Trade pieces, keep pawns, and invade with the rook.","do":"Trade minor pieces and keep pawns on the board.","dont":"Don't give back the exchange to win a pawn."},{"id":"end-86","part":"end","n":86,"m":"Queen and mixed endings","t":"Perpetual check: the queen's saving resource","p":[40],"x":"In queen endings the defender can often draw by checking forever. If the king can't escape the checks, it's a draw by repetition, even a pawn or two down.","do":"When worse in a queen ending, look for checks from different directions.","dont":"Don't let your king stand in the open when you're the side pressing."},{"id":"end-87","part":"end","n":87,"m":"Queen and mixed endings","t":"Queen vs pawns","p":[42],"x":"A queen easily beats pawns unless they are about to queen with their king supporting them. Bring your king to help.","do":"Use checks to gain time while your king approaches.","dont":"Don't let the pawns reach the 7th with their king near."},{"id":"end-88","part":"end","n":88,"m":"Technique and practical play","t":"Don't rush: improve everything first","p":[49],"x":"In a won ending, improve every piece and pawn before the decisive break. The defender can't improve, so there's no hurry.","do":"Ask: 'Can I improve anything before I push?'","dont":"Don't push the pawn as soon as you can."},{"id":"end-89","part":"end","n":89,"m":"Technique and practical play","t":"Schematic thinking","p":[49,9],"x":"Picture the ideal set-up for your pieces, then make the moves to reach it. Endings are about plans, not move-by-move tactics.","do":"Imagine where each piece should stand.","dont":"Don't calculate everything. Plan where pieces go."},{"id":"end-90","part":"end","n":90,"m":"Technique and practical play","t":"The two weaknesses principle in the endgame","p":[19],"x":"One weakness can be defended. Create a second, far away, and the defence breaks.","do":"When stuck, make a second target on the other wing.","dont":"Don't keep hitting the same defended pawn."},{"id":"end-91","part":"end","n":91,"m":"Technique and practical play","t":"Centralise everything","p":[41],"x":"In the endgame, central pieces reach both wings fastest. King to the centre first, pieces next.","do":"Bring the king to the centre at once.","dont":"Don't leave pieces on the edge."},{"id":"end-92","part":"end","n":92,"m":"Technique and practical play","t":"Exchange to simplify a win","p":[20],"x":"When ahead, trade pieces, not pawns. Every trade reduces his counterplay and makes your extra material count more.","do":"Offer trades when you're ahead.","dont":"Don't trade pawns when you're ahead. Each pawn is a winning chance."},{"id":"end-93","part":"end","n":93,"m":"Technique and practical play","t":"Defending: keep pieces on, trade pawns","p":[20,47],"x":"When behind, keep pieces on (for counterplay) and trade pawns (fewer pawns means more drawn positions).","do":"Trade pawns, especially on the side of his passer.","dont":"Don't trade pieces when you're down."},{"id":"end-94","part":"end","n":94,"m":"Technique and practical play","t":"Use zugzwang","p":[44],"x":"In endgames, the right waiting move can force him to give way. Look for positions where any move hurts him.","do":"Ask: 'If it were his move now, would he be worse?'","dont":"Don't make a committal move when a waiting move wins."},{"id":"end-95","part":"end","n":95,"m":"Technique and practical play","t":"Know the target positions","p":[47],"x":"Lucena, Philidor, the opposition, the wrong bishop and opposite-coloured bishops tell you what to aim for when you trade down.","do":"Before simplifying, ask which known position you'll reach.","dont":"Don't trade down blind."},{"id":"end-96","part":"end","n":96,"m":"Technique and practical play","t":"Endgame time management","p":[38],"x":"Endgames are where clocks run low. Learn basic positions until they're automatic, so you spend time on real decisions.","do":"Practise basic endgames against the engine until they're fast.","dont":"Don't spend your whole clock in the middlegame."},{"id":"end-97","part":"end","n":97,"m":"Technique and practical play","t":"Fortresses and drawing resources","p":[36,47],"x":"When losing, look for a fortress, the wrong bishop, opposite-coloured bishops, stalemate tricks or perpetual check. Half a point is better than zero.","do":"Ask: 'Which drawn position can I aim for?'","dont":"Don't resign while drawing resources exist."},{"id":"end-98","part":"end","n":98,"m":"Technique and practical play","t":"Passed pawns must be pushed","p":[42],"x":"A passed pawn grows stronger with every step and ties his pieces down. Push it when it's safe and supported.","do":"Escort the pawn with king and rook.","dont":"Don't push it into a blockade without support.","pr":"theme:advancedPawn"},{"id":"end-99","part":"end","n":99,"m":"Technique and practical play","t":"Create a passed pawn from a majority","p":[50],"x":"Advance the candidate pawn (the one with no enemy pawn in front) first. The others follow to support it.","do":"Push the candidate first.","dont":"Don't push the pawn that's opposed by an enemy pawn first.","pr":"theme:advancedPawn"},{"id":"end-100","part":"end","n":100,"m":"Technique and practical play","t":"Study classic endgames","p":[47],"x":"Capablanca, Rubinstein, Fischer and Carlsen won countless endgames with technique. Replay their endings in Guess the move.","do":"Replay Capablanca-Tartakower and Fischer-Taimanov.","dont":"Don't neglect endgames. They're where rating points are won quietly.","pr":"game:capablanca-tartakower-1924"}];
const COACH_NOTES=[{"t":"Play slower games","a":"Make 15+10 your main time control, with 30+0 when you can. Play fewer 10+0 games.","w":"Increment lets you use the thinking routine. Slow games build the calculation that fast games only test."},{"t":"Analyse before the engine","a":"After every game, find the turning point yourself and write one line on what you missed. Only then open the engine.","w":"The engine shows the answer. Your own analysis builds the skill. Players who go straight to the engine plateau."},{"t":"Keep a mistake journal","a":"One line per lost game: the move, the type of mistake (missed threat, loose piece, early pawn push, relaxed when winning).","w":"Your engine review found clear patterns. A journal tells you when they start to disappear."},{"t":"Play up, not down","a":"Seek opponents 100-200 points stronger. Losses to them teach more than wins against weaker players.","w":"Half your rapid games were against friends rated around 400. They don't test you."},{"t":"Stop after two losses","a":"Two losses in a row means stop for the day. No revenge games.","w":"Tilt is the biggest cause of rating swings, which you mentioned yourself (1600 to 2000 depending on the day)."},{"t":"Play when fresh","a":"No rated games when tired, late at night, or in a rush. Treat daily moves the same way.","w":"Your daily blunder rate (3.5 per 100 moves) is double your rapid rate. That points at attention, not skill."},{"t":"Train visualisation","a":"Solve some puzzles from the diagram only, without moving pieces. Replay model games in your head for the first 10 moves.","w":"Calculation is visualisation. If you can't see the position after three moves, you can't calculate it."},{"t":"Endgames are the cheapest points","a":"Spend at least 20% of study time on endgames, from Month 3 onwards.","w":"Few club players study them, so every endgame lesson wins points directly."},{"t":"Play over the board","a":"Join a local club or play a rated rapid tournament. A physical board changes how you calculate.","w":"Online ratings swing. An over-the-board rating is the real long-term measure, and you'll meet tougher, slower opponents."},{"t":"Read the classics","a":"Logical Chess Move by Move (Chernev), My System (Nimzowitsch), Silman's Complete Endgame Course, The Woodpecker Method, Chess Structures (Flores Rios).","w":"These books explain every move of whole games, which is the best way to build understanding at your level."},{"t":"Learn structures, not move lists","a":"For each opening, study 3 model games in its typical structure. Know the plans for 10-15 moves, not the moves for 25.","w":"Opponents leave theory early. Plans survive; memorised moves don't."},{"t":"Budget your clock","a":"Target: about a third of your time used by move 20, half by move 30. Spend it at critical moments, not on recaptures.","w":"You use only 14% by move 15 and never got into time trouble. You can afford to think more."},{"t":"Judge by averages, not today's rating","a":"Track your 30-game average and monthly change, not single sessions.","w":"Ratings fluctuate ±100 by chance. Averages show real progress and stop you panicking."},{"t":"Balance fast and slow puzzles","a":"About 1 Clock (speed) session to 3 slow, calculate-first sessions.","w":"Speed puzzles train pattern recognition. Slow puzzles train calculation, which is what rapid games need."},{"t":"Get a second pair of eyes","a":"Every three months, show 5 of your games to a stronger player or coach (or ask me to review them).","w":"Others see the habits you can't see in yourself."},{"t":"A pre-game routine","a":"Two minutes before each game: glance at your opening file, then say the Thinking Card out loud.","w":"Routines switch you into 'careful mode' from move one."},{"t":"Don't resign too early","a":"In daily and rapid, play on while there are tricks, stalemates or perpetual checks. But don't drag out dead-lost positions.","w":"Your opponents blunder too. Several of your daily losses were resigned in playable positions."},{"t":"Rest weeks","a":"Every 6-8 weeks, take a light week: only puzzles and one model game.","w":"Improvement consolidates during rest. Burn-out costs more than one light week."},{"t":"Physical health matters","a":"A short walk before a serious game, water, no heavy meal. Sleep before tournaments.","w":"Concentration over 60-90 minutes is physical. Strong players train it."},{"t":"Make the repertoire yours","a":"Add moves you meet in your games to your Knight'nMare repertoires after every game.","w":"A repertoire built from your own games covers exactly what your opponents play."}];
